"offense":[{"season":2019,"team":"PIT","distance":0.5453,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"CLE","distance":0.6506,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"GB","distance":0.6672,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Matt LaFleur"},{"season":2022,"team":"JAX","distance":0.6833,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"WAS","distance":0.6835,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2022,"team":"MIN","distance":0.5351,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"DEN","distance":0.704,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"},{"season":2025,"team":"SF","distance":0.7178,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Robert Saleh"},{"season":2020,"team":"LA","distance":0.838,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.84,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bears went 12-4 in 2018 and outscored opponents 421 to 283. The defense did most of the work, finishing 1st in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 14th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd highest shotgun rate and the 2nd highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Bears offense was a little above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd highest shotgun rate in the league. Mitchell Trubisky ran the passing game, with Allen Robinson leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bears had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 3rd or better in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per play, where it led the league, and it had no real weak spot. Khalil Mack led the team with 12.5 sacks, and Eddie Goldman added 3 despite playing only 53% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Bears were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 77% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 25% of the money on the books.","players":"Mitchell Trubisky played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,223 yards. Targets were spread around, with Allen Robinson and Taylor Gabriel leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Jordan Howard handled most of the work in the backfield with 65% of the carries. On defense, Khalil Mack led the team with 12.5 sacks and Kyle Fuller picked off 7 passes.","coaching":"Matt Nagy called the offense as head coach and Vic Fangio called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2019 Steelers and the 2019 Browns. Their defense looked most like the 2022 Vikings and the 2019 Broncos under Vic Fangio.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Bears changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}